//! check: run(exit=trap(assert), stdout="inner inner-defer before-trap")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: conf.trap.exit, mem.model.order
//!
//! Issue #209: `[conf.trap.exit]` ruled the PROC path (s132/D68 — a
//! trap inside a proc runs no defer below the boundary) and was silent
//! about the root domain, so nothing anywhere pinned whether a
//! root-domain trap flushes its pending defers on the way out. is33
//! left lupin's root path untouched for exactly that reason, and the
//! differential could not see the question: on a trapping program the
//! reference interpreter's record reports no stdout at all
//! (`stdout_inline: null`), so the two machines are verdict-identical
//! whatever they print.
//!
//! The ruling is the consistent one — a trap is not an error value and
//! runs NO defers anywhere; death at the root is immediate, matching
//! the killed-proc sequence (`conc/proc_cap_fault_join.lu` pins the
//! same shape one boundary down). So `root-defer` never reaches
//! stdout.
//!
//! The inner block is the control: its defer runs, at the inner
//! block's own exit, BEFORE the trap. Without it a green here could
//! not tell "the pending defer was abandoned" from "defers do not run
//! in this program at all".
//!
//! MEASURED DIVERGENCE, r05 (for is34). All three wolfc lanes —
//! `--checked`, `--native`, `--release` — print exactly
//! `inner inner-defer before-trap` and stop, which is the sentence
//! above. lupin 0.1.22 (pin 2bfbe5e) prints
//! `inner inner-defer before-trap root-defer`: the root-domain trap
//! runs the pending defer on its way out. Same verdict on both
//! (`trap(assert)`), different observable behaviour — the divergence
//! this file exists to make visible, since the record cannot carry it.

fn main() -> !int {
    defer print_raw(" root-defer")        // pending at the trap — must NOT run
    {
        defer print_raw(" inner-defer")   // runs at THIS block's exit
        print_raw("inner")
    }
    print_raw(" before-trap")
    let n = 2
    assert(n == 3)                        // TRAP assert
    print_raw(" unreachable")
    0
}
